course: dictionary
lesson: 3358
title: Exchange: a swap, a talk, or a change of money
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Exchange has more than one meaning, so the sentence around it tells you which one you need. «A landline phone sends the call along a wire or fibre to an exchange and on to the other phone.» Here an exchange is a place where calls are switched. Ask yourself: is something being given and something got back?
explain: To exchange means to give one thing and get another thing back. «To spend it you first swap it at a bank or exchange shop» means changing money. A «100-hryvnia note from Gran in Kyiv» will buy crisps in Kyiv, but a London shop needs pounds, so it has to be swapped first.
explain: Check the meaning by putting your idea into the sentence. «Anyone online offering you things in exchange for a photo is not a friend.» Does "in swap for a photo" make sense? Yes, so the meaning fits. Then the sentence tells you what to do: stop, don't reply, and tell an adult.
fact: Exchange can be a noun (a swap, or a short talk) or a verb (to swap something for something else).
fact: To exchange means to give one thing and get another thing back.
fact: To exchange money means changing it into the money of another country.
fact: An exchange can also be a short talk between people, or a school exchange where pupils visit another country.
fact: Historians call the two-way trade of plants, animals, people and germs across the ocean after 1492 the «Columbian Exchange».
exercise: Maths: Mia gives 4 stickers and gets 1 card. What is the exchange here? | *Mia gives 4 stickers and gets 1 card | Mia loses all her stickers | Mia buys a new album | Mia gets 4 stickers and gives 1 card
exercise: «To spend it you first swap it at a bank or exchange shop.» What is an exchange shop? | A shop that sells only books | *A shop that changes money into another country's money | A shop where you borrow toys | A shop that is always closed
exercise: «Anyone online offering you things in exchange for a photo is not a friend.» What should you do? | Reply quickly and send a photo | *Stop, don't reply, and tell an adult | Ask them for more things | Keep the photo secret and wait
test: «After 1492 plants, animals, people and germs crossed the ocean both ways.» What do historians call this two-way trade? | *The Columbian Exchange | The Corn Exchange clock | The Sun time | The landline
test: «Ben and Ruth had a brief exchange at the gate.» What does exchange mean here? | *A short talk between people | Running away | A kind of coin | A large box
exam: Which sentence uses exchange as a verb? | The exchange connects the phone calls in the city | Grandad had a quick exchange with the postman | *Lena will exchange her blue jumper for a green one at the shop | The school exchange sends pupils to France
